`recover_published` returns `Err` on any observation failure (recovery.rs:201 `RecoveryValidity::Unavailable(error) => return Err(error)`), where `Unavailable` is produced by `self.git.current_branch(path)` failing (recovery.rs:226-230). `recover_attempt` deliberately does NOT propagate the same condition — it quarantines and returns `Ok(())` (recovery.rs:151-155).

crates/fleet-daemon/src/services/worktrees/post_create.rs:97-103
```rust
context.progress("post-create runner detached")?;
let process = context
    .spawn_detached_child(Arc::clone(&self.shell), command, &intent.log_path)
    .await?;                       // hooks are now running
intent.pid = Some(process.pid);
self.write_post_create_intent(&intent)?;   // pid reaches disk only here
```
Recovery keys the decision purely on that persisted pid (post_create.rs:216-222):
```rust
if intent.pid.is_some() || service.files.exists(&intent.status_path) {
    service.finish_detached_post_create(intent).await
} else {
    service.launch_detached_post_create(intent, &context, RUNNER).await
}
```
The status file the `else` branch also checks is written only by the runner's very last statement (`RUNNER`, post_create.rs:19-21: `mv "$tmp" "$FLEET_STATUS_PATH"`), so it is absent for the whole run.
The clone path solves exactly this by having the child itself write its pid file before `exec` (crates/fleet-daemon/src/adapters/git.rs:139-143: `printf '%s\n' "$$" > "$temporary"` / `mv "$temporary" "$pid_file"` / `exec git clone ...`).

crates/fleet-daemon/src/services/worktrees/post_create.rs:110-114
```rust
if let Some(pid) = intent.pid {
    while pid_is_alive(pid) {
        tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(50)).await;
    }
}
```
`pid_is_alive` reports liveness for processes the daemon does not even own (crates/fleet-daemon/src/adapters/process.rs:159-166):
```rust
let result = unsafe { libc::kill(pid.cast_signed(), 0) };
result == 0 || std::io::Error::last_os_error().raw_os_error() == Some(libc::EPERM)
```
The recovery job that calls it is submitted non-cancellable and non-retryable (post_create.rs:214 `JobPolicy::new(false, false)`), and `finish_detached_post_create` takes no `JobCtx`, so nothing can interrupt the loop. Contrast `WatchDiscovery`, which never trusts a bare pid and keys everything on `ProcessIdentity { pid, start_identity }` (crates/fleet-daemon/src/adapters/process.rs:30-41).

